 classical proof of this theorem proceeds as follows (see [1], [5]). First one shows\nthat every principal minor of L is equal to the sum of the squares of the maximal minors\nof the signed vertex-edge incidence matrix of G. Then one computes that such a minor\nis equal to ±1 if the edges of G corresponding to the columns of the submatrix span a\ntree. Finally, one veriﬁes that the maximal principal minor is precisely 1/n times the\nproduct of the nonzero eigenvalues of L using the characteristic polynomial.\nMany generalizations of the theorem exist including for weighted graphs, simplicial\ncomplexes, and regular matroids.\nIn the last case, the regular matroid matrix tree\ntheorem is the following particular case of Theorem 3 in [7].\n2010 Mathematics Subject Classiﬁcation. Primary 52B40; Secondary 52C40.\nKey words and phrases. Regular matroid, unimodular matrix, cocircuit lattice, zonotope.\nBoth authors were partially supported by the project MINECO MTM2012-30951/FEDER. The ﬁrst\nauthor received additional support from the MCINN grant BES-2010-030080. The second author received\nadditional support from grants EUI-EURC-2011-4306, MTM 2011-24097 and 2009-SGR-1040.\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) A Polyhedral Proof of the Matrix Tree Theorem (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/261672712_A_Polyhedral_Proof_of_the_Matrix_Tree_Theorem)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.6 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; note here that in this paper we reserve the term lattice for a free discrete subgroup of a ... A POLYHEDRAL PROOF OF THE MATRIX TREE THEOREM 3 ... A POLYHEDRAL PROOF OF THE MATRIX TREE THEOREM 7 ... done without use of the Cauchy-Binet Theorem nor divisor theory on graphs. ... eigenvalue of L of multiplicity 1, and it is easy to check that the all-ones vector 1 n is a ... a conjecture.\nIt follows that 0 is an\n\neigenvalue of L of multiplicity 1, and it is easy to check that the all-one